WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Authentication.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.900
Before your API guards anything, it must know who is calling, and what they may do.

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.367
Here are the two gates.

4
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:10.900
A request arrives.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.201
Authentication asks: who are you?

6
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:15.567
Fail it, and the answer is four oh one.

7
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:18.160
Authorization asks: what may you do?

8
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.600
A known user above their pay grade gets four oh three.

9
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.367
Getting these right is free.

10
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:26.800
Two strategies keep a user logged in.

11
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:31.433
Sessions keep a random ID in a cookie and look the user up server side.

12
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:34.933
Tokens carry the state with the client, so there's no lookup.

13
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:38.613
Sessions are easy to revoke but need shared storage.

14
00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:40.433
Tokens are harder to revoke.

15
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:43.600
Now the iron rule for passwords.

16
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.467
Add a random salt, so identical passwords hash differently.

17
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:51.200
Run a deliberately slow hash like bcrypt or argon2.

18
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.852
Store only that hash.

19
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.233
Never encrypt, never keep plain text.

20
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:58.033
At login, re-hash the attempt and compare.

21
00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:00.433
Here it is in code.

22
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.449
Verifying a wrong guess against an argon2 hash simply returns false.

23
00:01:05.449 --> 00:01:06.767
No decryption ever.

24
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.233
With no user, we raise four oh one.

25
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:11.333
With the wrong role, four oh three.

26
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:13.767
Here's a real case.

27
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.567
Your reindex endpoint rebuilds the vector index, forty minutes of compute.

28
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.903
Anonymous calls get four oh one.

29
00:01:20.903 --> 00:01:23.385
A logged in analyst gets four oh three.

30
00:01:23.385 --> 00:01:25.167
Only two admins get through.

31
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:30.567
Because the check is a dependency, every future admin endpoint gets the same gate.

32
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.430
The classic mistake is encrypting passwords.

33
00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.367
A leaked key un-hides every one.

34
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:42.067
Store salted slow hashes, so even a full leak forces attackers to grind each password.

35
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.020
Let's recap.

36
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.533
Authentication is who, and failing it gives four oh one.

37
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:51.067
Authorization is what, and failing it gives four oh three.

38
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.400
Sessions are revocable, while tokens are stateless.

39
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.129
And passwords are only ever salted, slow hashes.

40
00:01:58.129 --> 00:01:59.200
Next up: OAuth.
